'''Greenway''', also known as '''Greenway House''', is an estate on the [[River Dart]] near [[Galmpton, Torbay|Galmpton]] in [[Devon]], England.  Once the home of the author [[Agatha Christie]], it is now owned by the [[National Trust]].

The estate was served by the [[Dartmouth Steam Railway]], with trains from [[Paignton]] and [[Kingswear]] stopping at [[Greenway Halt railway station (Devon)|Greenway Halt]] station. However, since March 2020, no services have stopped here.

==History==
===Location and early development: up to 1938===
Greenway is located on the eastern bank of the [[Tidal river|tidal]] [[River Dart]], facing the village of [[Dittisham]] on the opposite bank.{{sfn|Burdett|2010|p=4}} The estate is two miles from [[Galmpton, Torbay|Galmpton]], the nearest village,{{sfn|"Travelling to Greenway". National Trust}} and is in the [[South Hams]] district of the English county of [[Devon]].{{sfn|Historic England|1108548}} Greenway is three miles north of Dartmouth.{{sfn|Andrews|2017|p=101}} An early history book of Devon described Greenway as "very pleasantly and commodiously situated, with delightsome prospect to behold the barks and boats".{{sfn|Gosling|1911|p=11}}

Greenway was first mentioned in 1493 as "Greynway", the crossing point of the Dart to [[Dittisham]].{{sfn|''Proceedings at the Fifty-seventh Annual Meeting''. The Devonshire Association for the Advancement of Science, Literature and Art|p=36}} In the late 16th century, a [[Tudor style architecture|Tudor]] mansion called Greenway Court was built by Otto and Katherine Gilbert, members of a Devon seafaring family. One of the family's ships was named ''The Hope of Greenway'' and, according to Sara Burdett's history of the estate, it is probable that they kept their ships moored in the river.{{sfn|Burdett|2010|p=9}} The couple had three sons, all born at Greenway. In 1583, one son, Sir [[Humphrey Gilbert|Humphrey]], took possession of [[Newfoundland]] for [[Elizabeth I]], while his brother, Sir John, lived at Greenway. Humphrey and John's [[half-brother]], Sir [[Walter Raleigh]], also lived at the house.{{sfn|Scherman|Wilcox|1944|p=68}}{{sfn|"Churston Ferrers and Galmpton". Torbay Council}} In 1588, John was given the responsibility of 160 [[prisoners of war]] captured during the [[Spanish Armada]]; he put them to work on the estate, levelling the grounds.{{sfn|Historic England|1108548}}{{sfn|Rapple|2004}}

Little is known about the original Tudor building, although given the status of the family, Burdett considers it was "probably designed on a grand scale".{{sfn|Burdett|2010|p=9}} An archaeological examination of the current house's hallway shows evidence of a Tudor courtyard underneath.{{sfn|Burdett|2010|p=9}} Remnants of the Tudor slipway from the boathouse can be seen at low tide.<ref>{{Cite web |last=National |first=Trust |date=1 September 2020 |title=Behind the scenes at Greenway, Agatha Christie's holiday home, cared for by the National Trust |url=https://youtube.com/n4o_vr0xJHE |access-date=2 February 2021 |website=YouTube (google)}}{{Dead link|date=June 2024 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref>

In around 1700, the Gilberts made nearby [[Compton Castle]] their family seat and sold Greenway to Thomas Martyn, a resident of [[Totnes]], also in Devon. Over the next 90 years, the house passed down through the Roopes—the family of Martyn's wife—until it was bequeathed to a distant relation of the family, Roope Harris, on the provision that he changed his surname to Roope. Roope Harris Roope, as he became, built what is now the existing house in [[Georgian architecture|Georgian style]].{{sfn|Burdett|2010|pp=9–10}} Roope sold Greenway in 1791, to one Edward Elton for over £9,000.{{efn|£9,000 in 1791 equates to approximately £{{formatnum:{{Inflation|UK|9000|1791|r=-4}}}} in {{inflation/year|UK}}, according to calculations based on [[Consumer Price Index (United Kingdom)|Consumer Price Index]] measure of inflation.{{sfn|Clark|2018}}}} Roope went bankrupt in 1800; Burdett opines that this could have been because of the amount of money spent on rebuilding Greenway.{{sfn|Burdett|2010|p=10}}

[[File:Ordnance Survey map of Devon, 1854, showing the Greenway Estate.jpg|thumb|upright=1.3|left|1854 [[Ordnance Survey]] map showing the house at Greenway]]
The Elton family developed the garden, with some remodelling by the [[landscape gardener]] [[Humphry Repton]]. At some point in the late 18th century the Tudor house was entirely demolished. Burdett considers it was possibly Roope,{{sfn|Burdett|2010|p=10}} while [[Historic England]] think it was more likely to have been Edward Elton.{{sfn|Historic England|1108548}} Elton's son, James, took possession of the house upon the death of his father in 1811, and expanded the property, adding two [[Wing (building)|wings]] to the house, for a dining room and drawing room. He also paid for a new road from Galmpton to Greenway ferry, which changed the access to the estate. When he sold the estate in 1832, it was much changed, with a large kitchen garden, swimming pool{{Citation needed|date=January 2025|reason=Swimming pools were not common at country houses at this time}}, boat house and redesigned gardens. The estate was purchased, although only briefly, by a Sir Thomas Dinsdale, but was soon sold for £18,000 to Colonel Edward Carlyon,{{efn|£18,000 in 1832 equates to approximately £{{formatnum:{{Inflation|UK|18000|1832|r=-4}}}} in {{inflation/year|UK}}, according to calculations based on [[Consumer Price Index (United Kingdom)|Consumer Price Index]] measure of inflation.{{sfn|Clark|2018}}}} whose family owned [[Tregrehan House]], in [[Cornwall]]. The Carlyons did not make any significant alterations to the interior of the house; but, according to Burdett, they are likely the owners who introduced a [[rockery]] to the slope on the east of the house.{{sfn|Historic England|1108548}}{{sfn|Burdett|2010|p=11}}

[[File:Greenhouse in one of the walled gardens at Greenway - geograph.org.uk - 1540147.jpg|thumb|upright=1.3|A greenhouse in the walled garden, installed by Richard Harvey]]
Carlyon inherited Tregrehan House in 1842, and moved there the following year. Greenway was let out to a series of tenants until it was sold twice in quick succession, the last time to Richard Harvey—a Cornish copper and tin magnate—and his wife Susannah.{{sfn|Burdett|2010|pp=11–12}} The Harveys developed the estate extensively, restoring the stables and lodge house, installing two new [[greenhouse]]s and redecorating the interior. They restored much of Galmpton, including building the village school and the Manor Inn; Harvey also acquired the [[Lordship of the manor]] of Galmpton. Harvey died in 1870, his wife in 1882; they had no children to pass the estate on to, and it was sold for £44,000 to [[Thomas Bedford Bolitho]].{{efn|£44,000 in 1882 equates to approximately £{{formatnum:{{Inflation|UK|44000|1882|r=-4}}}} in {{inflation/year|UK}}, according to calculations based on [[Consumer Price Index (United Kingdom)|Consumer Price Index]] measure of inflation.{{sfn|Clark|2018}}}} Bolitho, the industrialist MP for [[St Ives (UK Parliament constituency)|St Ives]], added what Burdett describes as "a Cornish influence"{{sfn|Burdett|2010|p=13}} on the gardens, introducing plants such as [[camellia]]s, [[magnolia]]s, [[rhododendron]]s and [[Lauraceae|laurels]]. He built a new east wing to the house in 1892, which included a billiard room, study and bedrooms; this was demolished in 1938. Bolitho died in 1919 and the house passed to his daughter Mary, and her husband, Charles Williams, whose family owned [[Caerhays Castle]] near [[St Michael Caerhays]], Cornwall. Between them, the couple added several new varieties of plants from [[Plant nursery|nurseries]] in Cornwall. In 1937, they returned to Cornwall and sold the estate to [[Goodson baronets|Alfred Goodson]]. He split up the estate and sold it off the following year. The house, with {{convert|36|acre|ha}} of land, was available for sale for £6,000.{{sfn|Historic England|1108548}}{{sfn|Burdett|2010|p=13}}{{efn|£6,000 in 1938 equates to approximately £{{formatnum:{{Inflation|UK|6000|1938|r=-4}}}} in {{inflation/year|UK}}, according to calculations based on [[Consumer Price Index (United Kingdom)|Consumer Price Index]] measure of inflation.{{sfn|Clark|2018}}}}

===Agatha Christie and later: 1938 onwards===
In 1938, the writer [[Agatha Christie]] and her husband, the [[Archaeology|archaeologist]] [[Max Mallowan]], were becoming disenchanted with their home in nearby [[Torquay]]. The town had changed in the previous years, and the once uninterrupted view of the sea from the house became obstructed with new buildings.{{sfn|Hack|2009|p=152}} Looking around south Devon, Christie saw Greenway was available. She had seen the property during her youth and always thought it "the most perfect of the various properties on the Dart".{{sfn|Christie|1990|p=496}} In her later autobiography she wrote:

<blockquote>One day we saw that a house was up for sale that I had known when I was young&nbsp;... So we went over to Greenway, and very beautiful the house and grounds were. A white Georgian house of about 1780 or 90, with woods sweeping down to the Dart below, and a lot of fine shrubs and trees – the ideal house, a dream house.{{sfn|Christie|1990|pp=496–497}}</blockquote>

The house was occupied by Christie and Mallowan until their deaths in 1976 and 1978 respectively, and featured, under various guises, in several of Christie's novels. Christie's daughter, [[Rosalind Hicks]], and her husband, Anthony, lived in the house from 1968 until Rosalind's death in 2004.

[[Image:DSCN2353Greenway.jpg|thumb|upright=1.3|The house in July 2008, under restoration]]
The Greenway Estate was acquired by the [[National Trust]] in 2000 for its significance as a [[writer's home]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/travel/artsandculture/4797440/Agatha-Christies-home-Greenway-opens-to-the-Devon-public.html |title=Agatha Christie's home Greenway opens to the Devon public |first=Sophie |last=Campbell |date=24 February 2009 |work=The Daily Telegraph|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090227125933/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/travel/artsandculture/4797440/Agatha-Christies-home-Greenway-opens-to-the-Devon-public.html|archive-date=27 February 2009}}</ref> On 21 May 1985, Greenway House was made a Grade II* [[listed building]] by [[English Heritage]] (now [[Historic England]]) under list number 1108548.{{sfn|Historic England|1108548}} The ''[[Pevsner Architectural Guides|Pevsner]]'' guide for Devon describes it as "tall, [[Georgian architecture|late-Georgian]], [[stucco]]ed".{{sfn|Pevsner|Cherry|1991|p=525}} The gardens and parkland are Grade II listed in the [[National Register of Historic Parks and Gardens]].{{sfn|Historic England|1001686}} The house and gardens are open to the public, as is the Barn Gallery. The large riverside gardens contain plants from the southern hemisphere, whilst the Barn Gallery shows work by contemporary local artists.

[[File:Vinery, Greenway House - geograph.org.uk - 191217.jpg|thumb|upright|The Vinery]]

==Inspiration for Agatha Christie's works==
Agatha Christie frequently used places familiar to her as settings for her plots. Greenway Estate and its surroundings in their entirety or in parts are described in the following novels:<ref>John Curran: "Agatha Christie's Secret Notebooks - Fifty years of mystery in the making", Paperback edition, HarperCollinsPublishers 2010, {{ISBN|978-0-00-731057-9}}</ref>

*''[[The A.B.C. Murders]]'' (1936)
The character Sir Carmichael Clarke, a wealthy man from [[Churston Ferrers|Churston]], is one of three victims to have a copy of the A.B.C. Railway Guide left by his body. Churston is two miles from Greenway Estate and the station before Greenway Halt on the steam railway line. Within the plot, the 'C' of 'A.B.C.' refers to Churston as well as the character's name.

*''[[Five Little Pigs]]'' (1942)
The main house, the footpath leading from the main house to the battery overlooking the river Dart and the battery itself (where the murder occurs) are described in detail since the movements of the novel's protagonist at these locations are integral to the plot and the [[denouement]] of the murderer.

*''[[Towards Zero]]'' (1944)
The location of the estate opposite the village of Dittisham, divided from each other by the River Dart, plays an important part for the alibi and a nightly swim of one of the suspects.

*''[[Dead Man's Folly]]'' (1956)
The boat house of Greenway Estate is described as the spot where the first victim is discovered, and the nearby ferry landing serves as the place where another murder victim is dragged into the water for death by drowning. Other places described are the greenhouse and the tennis court, where [[Ariadne Oliver|Mrs. Oliver]] placed real clues and red herrings for the "murder hunt". The lodge of Greenway Estate serves as the home of Amy Folliat, the former owner of Nasse House.

Greenway's house and gardens are also used as the setting for Agatha Christie's short story 'The Shadow on the Glass' in the collection ''[[The Mysterious Mr Quin]]'',<ref>Agatha Christie: "The Mysterious Mr Quin", Paperback edition, Penguin Books 1953-63, p. 29.</ref> first published in 1930, eight years before she bought the estate.

==Popular culture==
[[ITV (TV network)|ITV]]'s ''[[Agatha Christie's Poirot]]'' episode "Dead Man's Folly" was filmed at the house.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://investigatingpoirot.blogspot.ch/2013/11/episode-by-episode-dead-mans-folly.html |title=Investigating Agatha Christie's Poirot: Episode-by-episode: Dead Man's Folly |author=Eirik |work=investigatingpoirot.blogspot.ch}}</ref> The estate is the setting for the murder-mystery novel ''Death at Greenway''. The author, [[Anthony Awards|Anthony Award]] winner and Mary Higgins Clark Award winner [[Lori Rader-Day]], says she was inspired to write the novel after reading that the estate housed children who were war evacuees during WWII.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Fogarty |first=Mignon |date=December 2021 |title=An Interview with Lori Rader-Day |url=https://www.quickanddirtytips.com/an-interview-with-lori-rader-day |access-date=24 February 2022 |website=Quick and Dirty Tips (Macmillan Publishing Group, LLC.)}}</ref>

